From dc0a60ecd818b1f71855f84f4ac9fd7e5e18d524 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Patch Baseline <patch@local>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 22:07:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(diet-plan): make meal portions actually solve for
 calorie/protein targets
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Previously pickCandidateFoods used fixed hardcoded quantities regardless
of the user's goal or calorie target — a weight-loss plan and a
muscle-gain plan got identical food quantities with only a deviation
warning attached when they didn't match. This violates the core
'goal-adaptive, not a template with a label swapped' principle.

Now:
- Foods are tagged with a role (base / protein / other)
- Quantities are scaled to hit each meal's calorie target (clamped to a
  sane portion range)
- If protein still falls short after calorie-scaling, the protein-role
  item is boosted specifically (since protein-dense foods add protein
  without blowing the calorie budget as fast as uniform scaling would)
- A calorie-correction pass trims the base/starch item if the protein
  boost pushed the meal meaningfully over target

Added a test that specifically proves quantities (not just targets)
differ between a low-calorie and high-calorie goal, and that each
meal's actual subtotal lands within 15% of its target.
